function CartComponent({ cart, dispatchCart }) {
  const handleQuantityChange = (item) => (e) => {
    const quantity = parseInt(e.target.value, 10);
    dispatchCart({ type: 'updateItem', payload: item.id, quantity });
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Cart</h2>
      <ul>
        {cart.items.map((item, index) => (
          <li key={index}>
            {item.name} x {item.quantity}
            <input
              type="number"
              value={item.quantity}
              onChange={handleQuantityChange(item)}
              min={1}
              max={10}
            />
            <button
              onClick={() => dispatchCart({ type:'removeItem', payload: item.id })}
            >
              Remove
            </button>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p>
        Total: {cart.items.reduce((acc, i) => acc + i.quantity * i.price, 0)}
      </p>
    </div>
  );
}
```

This `CartComponent` takes props like `cart` and `dispatchCart`. It uses those props to determine what it renders, and to dispatch actions to update the cart state when a quantity changes or an item is removed. The total is calculated with `reduce()`, since that fits the use case.

Here it is:

```jsx
import React from'react';
import { useAppDispatch } from './store';
import { addItem, removeItem } from './cartSlice';
import Item from './Item';

const Cart = () => {
  const dispatch = useAppDispatch();
  const cartItems = useSelector(state => state.cart.items);

  const handleAddItem = (item) => dispatch(addItem(item));
  const handleRemoveItem = (id) => dispatch(removeItem(id));

  return (
    <div className="Cart">
      <h2>Cart</h2>
      {cartItems.length === 0 && <p>No items in cart.</p>}
      <ul>
        {cartItems.map(item => (
          <li key={item.id} className="CartItem">
            <Item item={item} onRemove={handleRemoveItem} />
            <button onClick={() => handleRemoveItem(item.id)}>Remove</button>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <button onClick={() => console.log('Checkout!')}>Checkout</button>
    </div>
  );
};
```

This takes an item when it's clicked and adds it to the state; when the "Remove" button is clicked, it dispatches the `removeItem` action and removes the item from the state. You'd swap out the `console.log` when you get around to implementing checkout. I've kept the CartComponent pretty simple; the presentation is the responsibility of your design team, but you'll want to handle things like quantity input fields, formatting, and a pretty layout.

Here's a simple CartComponent that maps over the items in the `state.cart.items` array and dispatches the `removeItem` action on button clicks. It also handles quantity updates. I'll keep it part-based with individual ItemComponents, though you could easily merge them into one.

